{"title":"2012 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 2b","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_2\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2012 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=T-1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The <i>prime factorization</i> of 144 is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/1/d51bb3db158166a782ab9cd802bc38b51f008a92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"167\" height=\"15\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/5/be541ec10e87cb878b3666f21e2bf68062c85dd5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{4} \\times 3^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/f/50ff9540650f0f95f80da2f6692fc8ee413e2eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$144$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> is a perfect square because it can be written in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/5/46597ed49c0211cd119d29e476076fa8cbf62739.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^{2} \\times 3) \\times (2^{2} \\times 3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"148\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>The prime factorization of 45 is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/8/8b89cfd0abdb5c9baec504a05f71501c8c45e70d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^{2} \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore, 45 is not a perfect square, but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/00386b7bac2de10decaaaeea05ddbe2601b1326f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45 \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /> is a perfect square, because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/f/33fa04c27e489573c63f46637e0619e4418accf4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45 \\times 5 = 3^{2} \\times 5^{2} = (3 \\times 5) \\times (3 \\times 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"286\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2b&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The product <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/3/6f31c6b2c52eb2d486579efcc0d91b5384fc0918.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$112 \\times u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"15\" /> is a perfect square. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is a positive integer, what is the smallest possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2b&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>From <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2a\" title=\"2012 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 2a\">part a</a>, the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/3/dd3907e89ce5b06f13d78589b5d92624a8b41a7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$112$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/0/81082664dca7784e687568391842024516e4eb98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{4} \\times 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />. We then see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> has an even power in the prime factorization, but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> doesn't, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> must be some multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> multiplied by a perfect square for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/b/acb2d34d077a4541e233ae244a3344e898267bc3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$112u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"14\" /> to be a perfect square.\n</p><p>Thus, the smallest possible value for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/1/94152541528ed008abea53ff30e18a07a3c37f51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"20\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017230502\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.012 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.019 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 73/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 535/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3479/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    8.097      1 2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2\n100.00%    8.097      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36975:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017230502 and revision id 285874. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}